---
// Figure 10: an invalidation overtaking the reply it concerns, and the placeholder that fixes it.
import SimFigure from '../shared/SimFigure.astro';
import Choice from '../shared/Choice.astro';
import './cluster.css';
interface Props { number: number }
const { number } = Astro.props;
---
<SimFigure number={number} id="cluster-race" title="An invalidation that arrives before the value it invalidates" controls="steps"
  caption="A sequence of steps, not a timed simulation, and not one I reproduced: the ordering needs two connections and unlucky timing. The race and the placeholder remedy are described in Redis's client-side caching reference. Left out: the near cache's expiry, which bounds how long the stale value lasts.">
  <Choice slot="params" name="guard" label="The near cache" value="plain"
    options={[{ value: 'plain', label: 'stores every reply' }, { value: 'placeholder', label: 'uses a placeholder' }]} />
  <Fragment slot="text">
    <p>Without a placeholder: App A reads product:42 on its data connection and receives invalidations on a second connection. Redis executes the read and sends its reply, then App B writes v2, and Redis sends the invalidation. The reply is slow on its connection, drawn as a sloping arrow, and the invalidation overtakes it. The invalidation arrives first, finds nothing to remove, and then the old reply, v1, arrives and is stored. Redis has already sent its one invalidation, so A serves v1 until its copy expires.</p>
    <p>With a placeholder: A marks product:42 as being fetched before sending the read. The early invalidation removes the mark, so when v1 arrives A sees the mark has gone and does not store it. Its next read fetches v2. A single RESP3 connection that carries both replies and invalidations avoids the race altogether, because messages on one connection arrive in order.</p>
  </Fragment>
</SimFigure>
<script>
  import { switchSequence } from './switch-sequence.mjs';
  import { slopeArrows } from './slope-arrows.mjs';
  import { raceSpecs } from './sequences.mjs';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-figure-id="cluster-race"]').forEach(root => {
    switchSequence(root, raceSpecs, 'guard');
    const chosen = () => (raceSpecs as any)[root.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('[data-param="guard"]:checked')?.value ?? 'plain'];
    slopeArrows(root, chosen);
  });
</script>
